**Read:** [Particle–substrate interactions — Don Rimai, edited by Goodarz Ahmadi](../sources/Particle-Substrate-Interactions-Rimai.lingua#r_67MEFYZ26RD2ERAEKSVC0ACCQM), part 2's image-force and localized-charge discussion; revisit [Coulomb force, superposition and a test charge](../../../physics/electromagnetism/electrostatics/Coulomb-force-superposition-and-test-charge.lingua#r_4BCJ9YGVJSTWDV9S67V2RDR1YD).

The earlier free-particle drift used qE. Near a boundary, the field itself can depend on the particle and surface. For an ideal point charge q at height h above an infinite grounded conducting plane in a uniform medium of permittivity ε, the image construction gives an attractive normal force magnitude q²/(16πεh²). Either sign of q is attracted in this model.

A dielectric particle touching a real insulating surface is a different geometry. Charge can be localized, transferred, trapped or screened; humidity and neighboring particles may change the result. An image-force estimate and a short-range contact law should not be added without checking whether their definitions already include overlapping interactions.

**Work:** Derive the ideal force using an image charge at distance 2h. Explain why the physical interaction energy includes the induced-boundary response and is −q²/(16πεh), rather than the full two-independent-charges energy. Differentiate it to recover the force. List the observations required before using this idealization for a charged manufacturing particle.

